A `<-- strip` array is a copy count with no square factorisation (a prime, usually).
It is reported rather than padded: a partly-filled row breaks the symmetry the array exists
to provide. If it matters, the fix is upstream -- a copy count with factors, which means a
different unit width, which sizing chose.

NOT MEASURED HERE: what any of this costs electrically. These are geometric rules, and the
re-expression is width-preserving, but finger count changes junction and gate perimeter.
Run verify_shaping.py to simulate before/after and get the spec delta.
